'''''Lippia dulcis''''' ([[synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''Phyla dulcis'')<ref name = powo/> is a species of [[Perennial plant|perennial]] [[herbaceous plant]] that is native to southern Mexico, the [[Caribbean]] (Cuba, [[Hispaniola]], and Puerto Rico), Central America, Colombia, and Venezuela.<ref name="GRIN">{{GRIN | access-date=22 January 2010 }}</ref> It is known by several common names, including '''Aztec sweet herb''', '''bushy lippia''', '''honeyherb''', '''hierba dulce''', and '''''tzopelic-xihuitl''''' ([[Nahuatl]]). Its buds are also sold as ''dushi'' or ''dulce'' (''sweet'' in [[Papiamento]] and Spanish respectively) buttons.

==Uses==
This plant has historically been used as a natural [[sweetener]] and [[Herbalism|medicinal herb]] in its native Mexico and parts of Central America. It was used by the [[Aztec]]s and introduced to the [[Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire|Spanish]] when they arrived.

The sweet taste is caused by a [[sesquiterpene]] compound called [[hernandulcin]], which was discovered in 1985 and named for Francisco Hernández, the Spanish physician who first described the plant in the sixteenth century.<ref>Vandaveer, C. [http://www.killerplants.com/weird-plants/20031204.asp What is the Aztec sweet herb?] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080517062631/http://www.killerplants.com/weird-plants/20031204.asp |date=17 May 2008 }} killerplants.com</ref> Use of ''Lippia dulcis'' has not become widespread because it also contains high levels of bitter compounds, especially [[camphor]].<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Attiaa M, Kimb SU, Ro DK |year=2012 |title=Molecular cloning and characterization of (+)-epi-α-bisabolol synthase, catalyzing the first step in the biosynthesis of the natural sweetener, hernandulcin, in ''Lippia dulcis'' |journal=Archives of Biochemistry and Biophysics |volume=527 |issue=1 |pages=37–44 |doi=10.1016/j.abb.2012.07.010 |pmid=22867794 |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0003986112002925 |url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{cite thesis |type=MSc |last=Hurd |first=Matthew |date=2016 |title=Biochemical Studies of Terpenoid Metabolism in Aztec Sweet Herb |publisher=University of Calgary |doi=10.11575/PRISM/25122 }}</ref>

